Give the workqueue admission gate a floor, so one core is not a deadlock.

notifyd is the clearest case and every other one has the same shape. Twelve
threads, all of them parked in __workq_kernreturn with no work. One of its
ports holding six messages, with a kqueue watching it, sent by SpringBoard.
The message arrived, somebody is listening for it, workers are standing
idle, and the kernel never hands one the job. SpringBoard waits for the
answer with a synchronous wait that has no failure path, which is why this
board has spent weeks looking like "synchronous XPC does not work".

The gate is workq_constrained_allowance():

    count = wq_max_parallelism[_wq_bucket(at_qos)];
    if (count > thactive_count + busycount) {
            count -= thactive_count + busycount;
            return MIN(count, max_count);
    }
    return 0;

wq_max_parallelism is filled in workq_open() from qos_max_parallelism(),
which is the online logical CPU count. This port boots with cpus=1, so the
whole expression reduces to "admit a thread only while no other constrained
worker is active or busy" -- and busycount counts threads that blocked
recently and have not yet been confirmed idle. On a single Cortex-A53
carrying an entire iOS userland, that condition is almost never clear. The
counter Apple wrote to keep cores from being oversubscribed becomes a lock,
and it closes on the one path that every XPC server in the system takes.

Nobody hits this on a shipping device, because no shipping device runs this
userland on one core.

So the parallelism gets a floor of four, independent of how many cores are
online, with wqpar= to change it and wqpar=0 to restore the stock behaviour.
Oversubscribing one core costs context switches; refusing to schedule at all
costs the system. The line printed at the same point reports the CPU count,
the value the gate would have used and the value it will use, so the next
boot says plainly whether this was the number that was wrong.
diff -ruN a/bsd/pthread/pthread_workqueue.c b/bsd/pthread/pthread_workqueue.c
--- a/bsd/pthread/pthread_workqueue.c	2026-10-24 11:54:21.438377264 +0300
+++ b/bsd/pthread/pthread_workqueue.c	2026-11-08 22:09:51.736547545 +0300
@@ -57,6 +57,7 @@
 #include <mach/vm_statistics.h>
 #include <machine/atomic.h>
 #include <machine/machine_routines.h>
+#include <pexpert/pexpert.h>   /* PE_parse_boot_argn, for the wqpar floor */
 #include <vm/vm_map.h>
 #include <vm/vm_protos.h>
 
@@ -1578,6 +1579,52 @@
 			    qos_max_parallelism(qos, QOS_PARALLELISM_COUNT_LOGICAL);
 		}
 
+		{
+			/*
+			 * Give the admission gate a floor that does not depend on
+			 * how many cores happen to be online.
+			 *
+			 * wq_max_parallelism is how many constrained worker threads
+			 * workq_constrained_allowance() will let run at once, and it
+			 * is the online logical CPU count. The test it guards is
+			 *
+			 *     if (count > thactive_count + busycount) { ...admit... }
+			 *     return 0;
+			 *
+			 * so with one core the gate admits a thread only while no
+			 * other constrained worker is active or busy -- and busycount
+			 * includes threads that blocked recently and have not yet
+			 * been confirmed idle. On a single Cortex-A53 running a whole
+			 * iOS userland that condition is almost never clear, and the
+			 * counter Apple wrote to stop cores being oversubscribed
+			 * turns into a deadlock: notifyd sits with twelve workers
+			 * parked in __workq_kernreturn and six messages queued on a
+			 * port a kqueue is watching, and every process waiting for an
+			 * answer from it waits for the rest of the boot.
+			 *
+			 * Oversubscribing one core costs context switches. Refusing
+			 * to schedule at all costs the system, so the floor wins.
+			 * wqpar=0 restores the stock behaviour.
+			 */
+			uint32_t floor = 4;
+			uint32_t was = wq_max_parallelism[_wq_bucket(WORKQ_THREAD_QOS_MIN)];
+
+			PE_parse_boot_argn("wqpar", &floor, sizeof(floor));
+			if (floor > 64) {
+				floor = 64;
+			}
+			for (thread_qos_t qos = WORKQ_THREAD_QOS_MIN;
+			    qos <= WORKQ_THREAD_QOS_MAX; qos++) {
+				if (wq_max_parallelism[_wq_bucket(qos)] < floor) {
+					wq_max_parallelism[_wq_bucket(qos)] = floor;
+				}
+			}
+			printf("workq: cpus %u, parallelism %u -> %u, constrained max %u\n",
+			    num_cpus, was,
+			    wq_max_parallelism[_wq_bucket(WORKQ_THREAD_QOS_MIN)],
+			    wq_max_constrained_threads);
+		}
+
 		wq_init_constrained_limit = 0;
 	}
 
